.TH WHATCHANGED 1 "November 2026" "whatchanged 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whatchanged \- show a timeline of what changed on a Linux machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whatchanged
[\fB\-\-since\fR \fIWHEN\fR]
[\fB\-\-until\fR \fIWHEN\fR]
[\fB\-\-only\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-root\fR \fIDIR\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Something broke and it worked yesterday.
.B whatchanged
puts everything it can find about what changed since then on one timeline:
package installs and upgrades, new kernels, boots and shutdowns, edited
config files, shell startup files, systemd units and crontabs.
.PP
It only reads what is already on disk: package manager logs, file
modification times and the login records. There is no daemon, nothing is
sent anywhere and nothing is modified. It does not need root; what it
could not read is listed at the end under "Skipped:" on standard error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-since\fR \fIWHEN\fR
Start of the timeline. The default is
.BR yesterday .
.I WHEN
can be
.BR today ,
.BR yesterday ,
a time ago such as
.BR 30m ,
.BR 2h ,
.B 3d
or
.BR 1w ,
a date
.RB ( 2024\-05\-01 )
or a date and time
.RB ( "2024\-05\-01 09:00" ).
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-until\fR \fIWHEN\fR
End of the timeline, in the same forms. The default is now.
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR \fILIST\fR
Show only some sources, as a comma-separated list of the names under
.B SOURCES
below, for example
.BR "\-\-only pkg,config" .
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fIDIR\fR
Look at the system mounted at
.I DIR
instead of the running one, for example a broken install mounted from a
rescue USB. Times are then shown in that system's time zone.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show a short help text.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version.
.PP
Options can also be written as
.BR \-\-since=2h .
.SH SOURCES
.TP
.B pkg
Installs, upgrades and removals from the dpkg, pacman and dnf logs,
including rotated and gzip-compressed logs, with the apt or pacman command
that made the change when the log has it.
.B \-\-only pkg
also selects
.B flatpak
and
.BR snap .
.TP
.B flatpak
Flatpak apps and runtimes installed or updated, system-wide and per user.
.TP
.B snap
Snaps installed or refreshed.
.TP
.B kernel
Installed kernels, marking the running one and those not booted yet.
.TP
.B boot
Boots (with the kernel version) and clean shutdowns, from wtmpdb's
.I /var/lib/wtmpdb/wtmp.db
or the classic
.IR /var/log/wtmp .
.TP
.B config
Files edited under
.I /etc
and each user's
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.BR shell " (or " env )
Shell startup files such as
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
.I ~/.profile
and
.IR /etc/profile.d .
Shown as
.B env
lines.
.TP
.B service
systemd units enabled, masked or edited, system and per user.
.TP
.B cron
System and per-user crontabs.
.SH OUTPUT
One line per change, grouped under a date line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
2024\-05\-01
  09:12  pkg     upgraded  mesa 24.1\-1 \-> 24.2\-1 (apt upgrade, by alice)
  14:40  config  /etc/pipewire/pipewire.conf edited
  15:02  service docker.service enabled
.RE
.fi
.PP
Timelines longer than 15 lines end with a count per kind. Times are local,
taken from
.B TZ
or
.IR /etc/localtime .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The timeline was printed, even if it is empty or some sources were skipped.
.TP
.B 2
The arguments were wrong.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ
Time zone for reading and showing times, for example
.BR "TZ=UTC whatchanged \-\-since 2h" .
.SH NOTES
Most sources go by file modification times. Restoring a backup,
.BR "cp \-p" ,
rsync or a git checkout can set old or new times, so a file can show up or
be missing for reasons other than an edit. Package logs that logrotate has
already deleted are gone; how far back packages go depends on the distro's
rotation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
whatchanged
whatchanged \-\-since 3d
whatchanged \-\-since 2024\-05\-01 \-\-until 2024\-05\-03
whatchanged \-\-since 1w \-\-only pkg,boot
sudo whatchanged \-\-root /mnt \-\-since 3d
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/majidninja/whatchanged
